{
  "requirement": "For a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, the review prompt MUST direct the reviewer to apply the guidance of {cite}`requirements.cli.distributed_guidance`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation. For a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, the review prompt MUST direct the reviewer to apply the guidance of `requirements.cli.distributed_guidance`. That cited requirement is context, so I did not reassess it.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_applies_the_distributed_guidance]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It reads the habeas-requirements skill's `references/review-prompt.md` through `review_prompt()`. It takes the `## Criteria` section with `section()`, which asserts the heading exists. It then uses `assert_text` to require the phrase \"for a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, apply the Do and Don't lists in the habeas skill's `references/distributed-requirements.md`\".\n\nThat phrase names both trigger conditions (`{sites}` and `{violations}` queries). It also gives a directive to apply the distributed-requirements guidance. `assert_text` fails with a message when the phrase is missing. So a prompt that omitted the instruction, dropped either query kind, or moved it out of the Criteria section would fail the test.\n\nThe requirement has no other modes, boundaries or failure cases. I found no gap.",
